<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · other</p><h1 class="enc-title">Council of Chalcedon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">451</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Council of Chalcedon was the fourth ecumenical council of the Christian Church, held in 451 at Chalcedon, across the Bosporus from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, under Emperor Marcian. It defined Christ as one person in two natures, divine and human, and raised the see of Constantinople close to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in rank. Its definition was rejected by the Miaphysite churches of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, a lasting division of Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/council-of-chalcedon.jpg" alt="Council of Chalcedon" width="320" height="284" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vasily Surikov · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fourth_ecumenical_council_of_chalcedon_-_1876.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
